•	p. 8: Thomas Allen found that communication frequency between engineers drops exponentially beyond 30 metres. Patent citations confirm the same pattern at city scale — within the US, a patent is 5–10× more likely to cite a same-city patent than chance predicts (Jaffe et al., 1993), and this localisation has not weakened despite improvements in communication technology (Li, 2014). Doubling urban density is associated with a 21% increase in patent activity (Ahlfeldt & Pietrostefani, 2019).
One area that could be strengthened is the intellectual lineage of the discussion on agglomeration, clustering and knowledge spillovers. Many of the concepts presented have a long pedigree. Marshall's work on industrial districts recognised more than a century ago that firms benefit from co-location through specialised labour markets, supplier networks and the diffusion of tacit knowledge. Porter's cluster theory subsequently extended this argument, emphasising the role of geographically concentrated ecosystems of firms, institutions and supporting organisations in driving innovation and competitiveness. More recently, Krugman and the New Economic Geography literature provided a spatial explanation for why economic activity concentrates in particular places and why peripheral economies face structural disadvantages. McCann's 2009 analysis applied these insights directly to New Zealand, arguing that the increasing importance of agglomeration, urban scale and connectivity was central to understanding New Zealand's productivity performance. 
From this perspective, spatial analysis is not a peripheral issue for New Zealand policy; it is fundamental. New Zealand is a geographically large country with a dispersed population, long internal distances and one of the most peripheral locations in the OECD. If Krugman and McCann are broadly correct, geography and spatial structure are not background conditions but core determinants of productivity, innovation and economic development. 
It is therefore striking that many of these ideas remain relatively absent from mainstream policy analysis despite having been articulated in the New Zealand context more than sixteen years ago. That delay may itself warrant reflection. The relative neglect of spatial economics, economic geography and agglomeration theory suggests a narrowness in the analytical frameworks commonly applied within parts of the public sector. If productivity is shaped by the interaction of place, scale, connectivity and capability, then insufficient attention to those dimensions may itself be part of the policy problem. A stronger acknowledgement of this intellectual tradition would therefore strengthen the paper and help situate the current discussion within a longer and well-established body of scholarship.
•	3.3. New Zealand became "metropolitanised" fast and late
The shift to migration-led growth resulted in a relatively rapid metropolitanisation of population growth. New Zealand urbanised, i.e. moved to towns rather than the countryside, around the same time as most comparator advanced economies but Auckland remained relatively small, with a population of X, or Y% of the national population, in 1990 [reference needed]. However, once migration became the primary driver, growth became highly concentrated in metropolitan areas.

The rise of Auckland was not simply a consequence of migration. It was the spatial expression of a much deeper economic transition. Deregulation, deindustrialisation, globalisation, the emergence of Asia, the growth of knowledge-intensive industries and the increasing importance of agglomeration all favoured larger and more connected urban centres. As economic activity reorganised around skills, knowledge, services and global networks, metropolitan areas became increasingly attractive locations for both firms and workers. The resulting concentration of population and economic activity was not an accident of demography but a predictable outcome of structural changes in the economy.
These same forces operated at multiple spatial scales. Within New Zealand they favoured Auckland and, increasingly, the upper North Island over many smaller regional centres. However, within an increasingly integrated Australasian economy following CER, Auckland itself occupied a relatively peripheral position compared with the larger metropolitan economies of Sydney and Melbourne. As trade, investment and labour markets became more integrated across the Tasman, higher-order corporate, financial and head-office functions increasingly gravitated towards the larger Australian agglomerations, which offered deeper labour markets, greater industry diversity, larger capital pools and stronger international connectivity. In this sense Auckland was simultaneously the winner and loser of metropolitanisation: it gained relative importance within New Zealand while facing competitive pressures from even larger metropolitan centres within Australasia. The metropolitanisation of New Zealand was therefore the spatial manifestation of both domestic and international economic restructuring.
If geography, agglomeration, connectivity and urban scale are major determinants of productivity, then failing to incorporate them into the core policy narrative was itself part of the problem.

3.5 Metro growth has occurred alongside a structural infrastructure deficit
New Zealand invests heavily in infrastructure by OECD standards (around ~5.8% of GDP annually), but outcomes are weak due to high renewals requirements (a large share of infrastructure stock reaching end-of-life), lagged provision relative to population growth, and inefficiencies in planning, sequencing, and delivery (Te Waihanga, 2026; New Zealand Productivity Commission, 2022).
Comment:  a further challenge may be the gradual erosion of institutional and delivery capability associated with a stop-start project pipeline. Periods of intense activity are often followed by periods of retrenchment, making it difficult to sustain skilled workforces, organisational capability, supply chains and accumulated expertise. As a result, capability is rebuilt repeatedly rather than retained and deepened over time. 
There is also a question as to whether highly fragmented and competitive delivery models are always optimal in a small, geographically dispersed economy. Under conditions of irregular demand, competition can sometimes produce capability thinning, with firms unable to sustain specialist skills, capital investment or long-term workforce development between projects. 
The Stronger Christchurch Infrastructure Rebuild Team (SCIRT) provides an alternative example. Faced with the need to rebuild large parts of Christchurch's horizontal infrastructure following the earthquakes, SCIRT adopted a collaborative delivery model that brought together public and private sector capability within a single programme. While established under exceptional circumstances, it demonstrated how continuity of work, integrated planning and sustained capability development can contribute to delivery at scale. The broader lesson may be that infrastructure performance depends not only on funding levels and governance arrangements, but also on the capability systems through which projects are delivered.
 The issue is not simply whether New Zealand spends enough on infrastructure. It is whether New Zealand has created the conditions for infrastructure capability to accumulate. In many sectors we have effectively adopted a model that assumes capability can be purchased from a market when required. The Christchurch rebuild showed that under some circumstances capability is something that must be actively built, coordinated and sustained. A capability lens therefore raises a different question: Are we optimising for project-level competition, or are we optimising for system-level capability?
Capital is drawn into accommodating growth rather than enhancing productivity
Comment: This section attributes capital misallocation primarily to growth dynamics and infrastructure constraints. Is there also a role for tax and financial system settings? New Zealand's long-standing tendency for capital to flow into housing and land is often explained as the interaction of population growth, constrained urban supply, credit availability and tax treatment. The current framing risks understating the extent to which policy settings may reinforce the allocation of capital towards property rather than productive business investment and technology adoption. However, its good to have this perspective. Growth, whether economic or population has COSTS that everyone seems to ignore.
This pattern is consistent with international evidence: high-value, knowledge-intensive services tend to concentrate in large metropolitan areas where agglomeration benefits are strongest, including through knowledge spillovers associated with a more highly-educated workforce (Ahlfeldt & Pietrostefani, 2019; Ahrend et al., 2015).[AM1.1]


The argument is consistent with the analysis in the MBIE–MFAT Long-term Insights Briefing. In particular, the Briefing notes that globalisation and technological change have led economic activity to become increasingly concentrated in large, globally connected urban centres, and that knowledge-intensive industries benefit from talent pooling, rapid feedback, collaboration and knowledge spillovers associated with clustering and agglomeration. The Briefing further argues that New Zealand's small scale, geographic isolation and dispersed population reduce access to these effects. The current discussion could potentially be strengthened by noting that this is not merely an urbanisation phenomenon, but reflects the increasing importance of knowledge-intensive production, tacit knowledge exchange and innovation ecosystems in modern economies. 
This  sentence is actually narrower than the LTIB. The LTIB's argument is not simply that large metropolitan areas outperform; it is that knowledge-intensive activity is increasingly organised around clusters, agglomerations and innovation ecosystems, whether those are metropolitan, regional or internationally connected. 

5.7 Weak secondary city system
Outside Auckland, New Zealand's urban system is characterised by smaller centres that have not developed the scale or connectivity required to replicate metropolitan productivity dynamics; New Zealand's urban system is concentrated rather than polycentric, with a second tier (Wellington, Christchurch) that is smaller and less functionally integrated than equivalent secondary cities in comparator countries such as Switzerland or the Netherlands.
Differences in long-run city growth are only weakly explained by initial industry mix, and New Zealand cities have tended to diversify rather than specialise. (Coleman et al., 2019) This limits the effectiveness of policies aimed at redistributing activity across places, as services-led concentration is unlikely to be reversed through regional interventions. (Coleman et al., 2019)
As a result, the secondary city system does not operate as a strong network of complementary urban centres, but as a set of relatively shallow labour markets with limited agglomeration effects.
Comment
I don’t think this is right, or supported by the evidence. I agree that New Zealand is not polycentric in the European sense. However, the spatial mapping work suggests New Zealand may be more polycentric in capability terms than in population terms than you suggest: 
•	Auckland = metropolitan scale, finance, corporate functions, technology, international connectivity.
•	Wellington = government, policy, professional services, screen, digital.
•	Christchurch = advanced manufacturing, aerospace, engineering, Antarctic science.
•	Hamilton = agritech, dairy science, food innovation.
•	Dunedin = health research, biotech, natural history media.
•	Nelson = aquaculture and marine industries.
That is not a Dutch-style polycentric urban system, but it is a network of complementary capability nodes.

 While Christchurch, Wellington and Hamilton are smaller than many international comparator cities, they exhibit distinct and nationally significant concentrations of capability. Christchurch e.g., has developed substantial strengths in advanced manufacturing, aerospace, engineering, Antarctic-related activities, agritech engineering and health technologies and significant inbtertaional connections through FDI and international R&D. 
From this perspective, the question is not whether New Zealand's secondary cities generate agglomeration effects comparable to Zurich, Amsterdam or Munich. They clearly do not. Rather, the question is whether they possess specialised combinations of firms, institutions, skills, infrastructure and knowledge that contribute disproportionately to innovation, productivity and diversification. The evidence increasingly suggests they do.
In particular, our spatial mapping work suggests that New Zealand's economic geography is characterised less by a simple Auckland-versus-regions dichotomy and more by a network of complementary capability centres. Auckland's role is to provide metropolitan scale, deep labour markets, international connectivity and frontier-firm potential. Christchurch, Wellington, Hamilton and other centres contribute different but complementary capabilities to the national economy. Their significance derives less from size than from capability depth and specialisation. Given NZ’s large geography it is not surprising that different regions have developed different sets of capabilities.
I therefore wonder whether the paper somewhat understates the extent to which agglomeration and knowledge spillovers exist outside Auckland. The issue may not be an absence of agglomeration effects, but rather the presence of multiple forms of agglomeration operating at different scales and serving different economic functions. A capability-centred lens potentially reveals a richer and more differentiated urban system than one focused primarily on metropolitan population size and labour-market depth.
The policy implication is important. If secondary cities are viewed primarily as shallow labour markets, policy naturally focuses on Auckland as the sole engine of productivity. If, however, these centres are understood as specialised capability nodes within a national system, the challenge becomes one of strengthening the connections, complementary functions and capability accumulation between them. That interpretation is more consistent with the spatial distribution of a number of New Zealand's emerging knowledge-intensive sectors and with the evidence from recent sector mapping work.

