Comment
I think this is one of the places where I would push back most strongly.
The statement:
"Improving national productivity does not require redistributing growth, but improving the performance of the metropolitan system through which that growth is delivered."
contains an important truth, but risks becoming too Auckland-centric.
Auckland clearly matters enormously. It contains roughly one-third of New Zealand's population and economic activity and is the country's only metropolitan economy of international scale. Improving Auckland's housing, transport, infrastructure and labour market performance would almost certainly lift national productivity. That part of the argument is persuasive.
However, it does not necessarily follow that Auckland's success automatically translates into productivity gains elsewhere in New Zealand.
One of the recurring findings from my spatial mapping work is that New Zealand's economy is characterised by substantial geographic distance and a range of specialised capability centres. Dunedin's health research and biotechnology capabilities, Christchurch's engineering and aerospace activities, Wellington's policy, professional services and screen sector, Hamilton's agritech ecosystem, Nelson's aquaculture capabilities and Tauranga's logistics functions are all embedded in local combinations of firms, institutions, skills and infrastructure. These capabilities often depend more on local ecosystem conditions than on Auckland's performance.
The further south one moves, the less obvious it becomes that Auckland's agglomeration benefits diffuse strongly across space. A thriving Auckland may have significant impacts on Auckland, moderate impacts on Hamilton and Tauranga, and weaker impacts on Christchurch or Dunedin. In a country with the geography of New Zealand, agglomeration effects are unlikely to operate evenly at the national scale.
This suggests a distinction between two propositions.
The first is:
Improving Auckland's performance is likely to improve national productivity.
I think that is largely correct.
The second is:
Improving Auckland's performance is sufficient to improve productivity throughout the national economy.
I am much less convinced by that proposition.
The spatial mapping evidence suggests that many of New Zealand's emerging knowledge-intensive sectors are not simply satellites of Auckland. They are located in specialised regional and secondary-city ecosystems that possess their own capability stacks, institutional arrangements and forms of agglomeration. Their future performance may depend as much on local skills pipelines, research capability, infrastructure, industry organisations and international connections as on developments in Auckland.
I would therefore suggest something closer to:
Improving Auckland's performance is likely to be one of the most powerful national productivity levers available to New Zealand. However, Auckland is not the sole locus of productive capability. New Zealand's economic geography also contains a range of specialised capability centres whose performance depends on local ecosystem conditions. A successful spatial strategy should therefore seek both to improve the functioning of Auckland as the country's primary metropolitan economy and to strengthen the specialised capability systems located elsewhere in the country.
That framing aligns better with both the capability-stack approach and the sector-mapping work. 
The choice is not really Auckland versus the regions. The challenge is understanding how Auckland's metropolitan functions interact with a wider network of specialised capability centres across New Zealand.
However, you core point that Auckland underperforms due to systemic reasons and this significant holds us back remains true.
