Your section 6. The political economy of the spatial system, and the importance of Auckland and the regions

My overall reaction is that this is probably the strongest part of the paper, if a little less academic. The central argument—that spatial policy should not be framed as a contest between Auckland and the rest of New Zealand—is both analytically sound and politically important.
However, I think there are three places where the argument could be strengthened.
First, the discussion is still a little too binary. You move from "Auckland versus the regions" to a more sophisticated Auckland-plus-regions framing, which is an improvement, but it still tends to flatten the economic geography that sits between those categories. 
One of the key insights from the spatial mapping work is that New Zealand is not simply Auckland and "the regions". It contains a series of specialised capability centres that perform different national functions. Christchurch is not simply a regional city; it is a nationally significant centre for advanced manufacturing, engineering, aerospace and Antarctic-related activities. Wellington is not simply another city; it is the country's primary centre for public sector capability (sic) , professional services, screen production and parts of the digital economy. Hamilton plays a distinct role in agritech, dairy science and food innovation. Dunedin has nationally important health research and biotechnology capabilities. The story therefore becomes richer if Auckland is framed as the dominant metropolitan centre within a wider network of specialised capability nodes.
Second, I think the paper slightly underplays distance. The argument that Auckland's productivity is a major determinant of national productivity is clearly correct. Auckland accounts for roughly one-third of New Zealand's population and economic activity, so its performance matters enormously. But there is a difference between saying Auckland's success is important for New Zealand and saying Auckland's success automatically benefits all places equally. 
Given New Zealand's geography, the agglomeration benefits generated in Auckland are unlikely to diffuse evenly to places such as Dunedin, Invercargill or Nelson. The spatial mapping work suggests that many capability systems are locally embedded and depend on local combinations of firms, institutions, skills and infrastructure. A thriving Auckland is therefore necessary but not sufficient. National productivity depends both on Auckland functioning well as a metropolitan economy and on specialised capability systems elsewhere functioning well in their own right.
Third, I think you are very close to a particularly important insight but do not quite make it explicit. The paper rightly rejects the idea that every place should perform the same economic role. However, the stronger argument is not simply that Auckland should be more productive than the regions. It is that different places contribute to national productivity through different mechanisms.
o	Auckland contributes through metropolitan scale, labour-market depth, international connectivity and frontier-firm potential.
o	Christchurch contributes through engineering, advanced manufacturing and technology capability.
o	Wellington contributes through government, professional services, digital capability and creative industries.
o	Regional New Zealand contributes through primary industries, tourism, natural resources, specialist industry clusters, environmental assets and cultural identity.
The objective of spatial policy should therefore not be to make every place look like Auckland, nor to redistribute growth away from Auckland. It should be to ensure that each part of the national capability system is able to perform the role it is best placed to perform. 
It is in my view much better to think about New Zealand as a series of regional economies with different histories, capabilities, levels of development, path dependencies, future potential and Tangata Whenua, with Auckland playing a particular and critical set of functions.
That, in my view, is where the capability stack concept becomes particularly useful. Auckland has the deepest metropolitan capability stack. Christchurch has a different but still nationally significant capability stack. Hamilton, Wellington, Dunedin and Nelson each have their own distinctive combinations of skills, institutions, firms, infrastructure and market connections. The question for spatial policy is how to strengthen and connect these capability stacks rather than treating spatial outcomes as a zero-sum competition between places.
So my feedback would be that you are directionally correct, but the paper becomes even stronger if it shifts from an "Auckland versus regions" framing to a "national network of differentiated capability centres" framing. That interpretation is more consistent with both the economic geography literature and the evidence emerging from the sector-mapping work.
You said: Spatial policy has often oscillated between supporting the growth of major centres and seeking to sustain opportunity across the wider regions"
My reading (and tragically memory)  of New Zealand economic history is almost the opposite.
For much of the twentieth century, economic policy was strongly oriented toward supporting spatially dispersed economic activity and maintaining viable regional economies. Import substitution, tariff protection, state investment, agricultural development programmes, transport policy, regional public services and decentralised government activity all contributed to sustaining employment and industrial capability in provincial centres. 
Places such as Masterton, Levin, Timaru, Invercargill and many others hosted manufacturing and service activities that have subsequently contracted or disappeared. The post-1984 reforms arguably represented a sharp break from this historical model, exposing regional economies to competitive pressures and accelerating metropolitan concentration. The historical transition may therefore be better characterised as a shift from a broadly dispersed economic geography towards a more metropolitan and globally integrated one, rather than a long-standing oscillation between the two approaches.

Many of those regional industries were not necessarily globally competitive, but they did represent local capability stocks. They supported:
•	technical skills,
•	management capability,
•	local supplier networks,
•	apprenticeships,
•	manufacturing know-how,
•	civic institutions.
The reforms did not simply redistribute economic activity. They also removed a large amount of regional productive capability.
That is why the story is not just one of urbanisation. It is also a story of: deregulation + deindustrialisation + globalisation + metropolitanisation + capability concentration.
In fact, I think one of the strongest historical interpretations is:
Until the 1980s New Zealand deliberately maintained a dispersed economic geography. After the reforms, economic activity increasingly reorganised itself around the locational logic of a small peripheral economy operating in a globalised and knowledge-intensive world. The rise of Auckland and the relative decline of many provincial centres was therefore not merely a demographic phenomenon; it was the spatial expression of a profound change in the structure of the economy.
That strikes me as more historically accurate than the idea that New Zealand has continually oscillated between regionalism and metropolitanism. The real discontinuity is the 1980s and 1990s. Before that, policy often actively supported regional economic development. After that, policy became much more spatially neutral in intent, even as its effects became increasingly spatially concentrated.
